1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:01,302
9:20 PM,

2
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:04,271
November 26, 2008.

3
00:00:05,547 --> 00:00:08,034
Ten men step off inflatable speedboats

4
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:09,688
onto the Mumbai shoreline.

5
00:00:10,063 --> 00:00:11,274
Each has a rifle.

6
00:00:11,652 --> 00:00:12,667
Each has a phone.

7
00:00:13,842 --> 00:00:16,394
In the next ten minutes, five attacks

8
00:00:16,407 --> 00:00:17,800
begin across the city.

9
00:00:18,217 --> 00:00:20,899
Over the next sixty hours, this becomes

10
00:00:20,912 --> 00:00:23,321
the longest hostage siege India has ever

11
00:00:23,334 --> 00:00:23,881
faced.

12
00:00:25,154 --> 00:00:27,120
But here is the part that still doesn't

13
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:27,563
fit.

14
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:29,985
The men pulling the triggers weren't the

15
00:00:29,998 --> 00:00:31,105
ones in charge.

16
00:00:31,495 --> 00:00:33,618
The orders came down a phone line, from a

17
00:00:33,631 --> 00:00:36,196
room six hundred kilometers away — where

18
00:00:36,209 --> 00:00:38,253
someone was watching the siege on live

19
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:38,943
television.

20
00:00:40,084 --> 00:00:42,649
So who was really holding Mumbai hostage?

21
00:00:50,024 --> 00:00:51,782
Everything that follows comes from the

22
00:00:51,795 --> 00:00:54,464
record — police and NSG timelines, court

23
00:00:54,477 --> 00:00:57,081
testimony, and the recordings themselves.

24
00:00:57,485 --> 00:00:59,373
And the record says this story doesn't

25
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:01,326
start on November 26th.

26
00:01:02,567 --> 00:01:04,351
It starts five days earlier.

27
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:07,363
On the night of November 21st,

28
00:01:07,871 --> 00:01:10,436
ten men slipped out of Karachi aboard two

29
00:01:10,449 --> 00:01:11,387
cargo vessels,

30
00:01:11,985 --> 00:01:13,991
the Al Fauz and the Al Hussaini.

31
00:01:14,407 --> 00:01:16,660
For roughly thirty-eight hours, the Indian

32
00:01:16,673 --> 00:01:17,936
Navy never saw them.

33
00:01:18,353 --> 00:01:21,139
Each man carried an AK-47 and eight

34
00:01:21,152 --> 00:01:21,816
grenades.

35
00:01:22,192 --> 00:01:24,197
And dried fruit, for the journey.

36
00:01:25,392 --> 00:01:26,681
On November 23rd,

37
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:28,908
at around 4:30 PM,

38
00:01:29,494 --> 00:01:31,499
they seized an Indian fishing trawler,

39
00:01:31,994 --> 00:01:33,244
the MV Kuber.

40
00:01:33,634 --> 00:01:35,236
They killed four of its crew.

41
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:37,671
The captain, they kept alive — to sail

42
00:01:37,684 --> 00:01:38,569
them to India.

43
00:01:39,848 --> 00:01:41,892
Within sight of Mumbai, they killed him

44
00:01:41,905 --> 00:01:42,296
too.

45
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,004
No one was left who could warn anyone.

46
00:01:46,188 --> 00:01:48,584
Except the warnings already existed.

47
00:01:49,001 --> 00:01:51,084
Indian intelligence had intercepted a

48
00:01:51,097 --> 00:01:53,779
satellite call on November 18th about a

49
00:01:53,792 --> 00:01:54,886
sea-borne attack.

50
00:01:55,262 --> 00:01:58,009
US intelligence had warned the Taj Hotels

51
00:01:58,022 --> 00:02:01,238
chairman, Ratan Tata, months before.

52
00:02:02,503 --> 00:02:03,323
Two warnings.

53
00:02:03,714 --> 00:02:05,889
Neither one changed what was waiting on

54
00:02:05,902 --> 00:02:06,501
the shore.

55
00:02:07,891 --> 00:02:09,987
They came ashore between 8:00  and

56
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:11,042
8:30 PM.

57
00:02:12,274 --> 00:02:14,435
Two landing points on Cuffe Parade:

58
00:02:14,774 --> 00:02:17,261
Macchimar Nagar and Badhwar Park.

59
00:02:17,667 --> 00:02:19,893
When curious locals asked who they were,

60
00:02:20,258 --> 00:02:21,573
they said they were students.

61
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:24,552
Then they split into pairs.

62
00:02:25,678 --> 00:02:28,035
Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus.

63
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:30,849
One of the busiest railway stations on

64
00:02:30,862 --> 00:02:31,383
earth.

65
00:02:31,759 --> 00:02:35,132
Ajmal Kasab and his partner, Ismail Khan,

66
00:02:35,431 --> 00:02:37,827
walked into the passenger hall and opened

67
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:38,361
fire.

68
00:02:38,753 --> 00:02:40,875
They threw grenades into the crowd.

69
00:02:41,292 --> 00:02:43,076
Fifty-eight people were killed here.

70
00:02:44,334 --> 00:02:46,482
It was the deadliest single site of the

71
00:02:46,495 --> 00:02:47,589
entire attack.

72
00:02:48,758 --> 00:02:51,323
Leopold Café, a few minutes south.

73
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:53,823
Two gunmen sprayed it with bullets and

74
00:02:53,836 --> 00:02:54,930
killed ten people.

75
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:56,818
Then they fled on foot.

76
00:02:57,209 --> 00:02:59,513
The explosives they'd left in two taxis

77
00:02:59,526 --> 00:03:00,607
killed five more.

78
00:03:01,783 --> 00:03:03,359
And at almost the same moment,

79
00:03:03,866 --> 00:03:05,494
pairs of gunmen walked into the

80
00:03:05,494 --> 00:03:08,150
Taj Mahal Palace and the Oberoi-Trident.

81
00:03:08,567 --> 00:03:10,885
Two five-star hotels that would become the

82
00:03:10,898 --> 00:03:12,603
main stage for three days.

83
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:16,036
The fifth target was one most of the world

84
00:03:16,049 --> 00:03:17,142
had never heard of:

85
00:03:17,637 --> 00:03:19,629
a small Jewish outreach centre called

86
00:03:19,629 --> 00:03:20,684
Nariman House.

87
00:03:21,859 --> 00:03:23,031
Five locations.

88
00:03:23,422 --> 00:03:24,216
Ten minutes.

89
00:03:24,592 --> 00:03:25,295
Ten men.

90
00:03:26,558 --> 00:03:28,550
This is what eighteen months of rehearsal

91
00:03:28,563 --> 00:03:30,998
looks like — the curriculum from our last

92
00:03:31,011 --> 00:03:32,404
file, finally deployed.

93
00:03:32,808 --> 00:03:35,204
But rehearsal alone doesn't explain what

94
00:03:35,217 --> 00:03:36,181
happened next.

95
00:03:37,578 --> 00:03:39,492
The gunmen weren't improvising.

96
00:03:40,754 --> 00:03:42,121
They were being directed.

97
00:03:43,352 --> 00:03:45,826
Intercepted wireless communications show

98
00:03:45,839 --> 00:03:48,040
them taking real-time instructions from a

99
00:03:48,053 --> 00:03:49,863
control room in Pakistan.

100
00:03:50,266 --> 00:03:52,324
That room was watching Indian television

101
00:03:52,337 --> 00:03:54,381
news — tracking troop movements and

102
00:03:54,394 --> 00:03:55,527
hostage positions,

103
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:58,105
then relaying it back to the men inside

104
00:03:58,118 --> 00:03:58,847
the hotels.

105
00:03:59,251 --> 00:04:01,100
The siege was being broadcast.

106
00:04:01,529 --> 00:04:03,821
And the broadcast was being used as

107
00:04:03,834 --> 00:04:04,706
intelligence.

108
00:04:05,123 --> 00:04:07,597
When Indian authorities realised it, they

109
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:09,967
cut the television feeds to the hotels.

110
00:04:11,159 --> 00:04:13,464
David Headley later testified that he

111
00:04:13,477 --> 00:04:15,664
recognised the voices in that room.

112
00:04:16,081 --> 00:04:18,594
His own handler, Sajid Mir.

113
00:04:19,838 --> 00:04:22,911
And two others, known as Abu Khafa and

114
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:24,018
Abu Al Kama.

115
00:04:24,436 --> 00:04:26,311
Recordings played in court reportedly

116
00:04:26,324 --> 00:04:28,069
captured the control room telling the

117
00:04:28,082 --> 00:04:30,725
gunmen that Mumbai's anti-terrorism chief

118
00:04:30,739 --> 00:04:31,728
had just been killed.

119
00:04:32,104 --> 00:04:34,669
News they heard from Karachi — about

120
00:04:34,682 --> 00:04:37,078
something happening a few kilometres away,

121
00:04:37,442 --> 00:04:39,200
outside Cama Hospital.

122
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:43,831
From CST, Kasab and Ismail Khan

123
00:04:43,844 --> 00:04:46,032
moved on foot to Cama Hospital.

124
00:04:46,451 --> 00:04:48,391
It was around 10:30 PM.

125
00:04:48,794 --> 00:04:50,279
The staff saw them coming.

126
00:04:50,669 --> 00:04:52,818
They locked patients into their rooms.

127
00:04:54,098 --> 00:04:56,286
That decision likely saved lives.

128
00:04:57,462 --> 00:05:00,418
Outside, three of Mumbai's most senior

129
00:05:00,431 --> 00:05:02,384
officers tried to stop them from a jeep:

130
00:05:03,217 --> 00:05:05,965
Anti-Terrorism Squad chief Hemant Karkare,

131
00:05:06,577 --> 00:05:08,986
additional commissioner Ashok Kamte, and

132
00:05:08,999 --> 00:05:11,082
senior inspector Vijay Salaskar.

133
00:05:11,473 --> 00:05:12,697
All three were killed.

134
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:15,366
The gunmen took the same police jeep and

135
00:05:15,379 --> 00:05:16,160
drove off.

136
00:05:16,551 --> 00:05:17,618
They didn't get far.

137
00:05:17,994 --> 00:05:20,989
Near Girgaum Chowpatty, officers from the

138
00:05:21,002 --> 00:05:23,528
Gamdevi police station stopped the jeep.

139
00:05:23,905 --> 00:05:25,754
Ismail Khan was killed.

140
00:05:27,011 --> 00:05:30,240
Kasab, wounded, was taken alive.

141
00:05:31,436 --> 00:05:33,585
He was the only one of the ten who would

142
00:05:33,598 --> 00:05:33,949
be.

143
00:05:34,366 --> 00:05:36,462
And that one survivor is the reason the

144
00:05:36,475 --> 00:05:39,171
world would later hear, in his own words,

145
00:05:39,379 --> 00:05:40,980
how the operation was built.

146
00:05:41,384 --> 00:05:44,053
By 1:00 AM, this thread of the night was

147
00:05:44,066 --> 00:05:44,509
over.

148
00:05:45,757 --> 00:05:47,971
The other four were just getting started.

149
00:05:49,357 --> 00:05:51,766
Four gunmen had entered the Taj at around

150
00:05:51,779 --> 00:05:53,355
9:44 PM.

151
00:05:53,745 --> 00:05:55,802
They moved through the lobby and up toward

152
00:05:55,816 --> 00:05:56,909
the grand staircase.

153
00:05:57,326 --> 00:05:59,188
According to a West Point case study of

154
00:05:59,201 --> 00:06:01,792
the siege, it was a position that let them

155
00:06:01,805 --> 00:06:03,615
watch the main entrance and everything

156
00:06:03,628 --> 00:06:04,201
below it.

157
00:06:05,467 --> 00:06:07,134
They had chosen the high ground.

158
00:06:08,305 --> 00:06:09,399
By 11:00 PM,

159
00:06:09,946 --> 00:06:11,730
police had surrounded the building.

160
00:06:12,133 --> 00:06:13,618
Then, at 1:00 AM,

161
00:06:14,217 --> 00:06:16,209
a blast tore through the central dome.

162
00:06:16,625 --> 00:06:18,813
Fire spread through the heritage wing.

163
00:06:19,230 --> 00:06:21,730
Army units arrived by 2:30 AM,

164
00:06:22,290 --> 00:06:24,047
and fire engines by three.

165
00:06:24,464 --> 00:06:25,167
Through the dark,

166
00:06:25,662 --> 00:06:27,758
firefighters raised ladders and pulled

167
00:06:27,771 --> 00:06:29,790
more than two hundred guests to safety

168
00:06:30,193 --> 00:06:32,198
while gun battles went on inside.

169
00:06:33,389 --> 00:06:35,668
By dawn, the fire was largely under

170
00:06:35,681 --> 00:06:36,293
control.

171
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:38,732
The siege was not.

172
00:06:39,162 --> 00:06:41,128
The gunmen had moved into the newer

173
00:06:41,141 --> 00:06:43,511
tower — holding somewhere between one

174
00:06:43,524 --> 00:06:44,696
hundred and one hundred and fifty

175
00:06:44,709 --> 00:06:45,438
hostages.

176
00:06:46,857 --> 00:06:49,735
On the morning of the 27th came a decision

177
00:06:49,748 --> 00:06:51,961
that has been criticised ever since.

178
00:06:52,378 --> 00:06:54,761
Local police were told to stand down and

179
00:06:54,774 --> 00:06:56,818
hand full authority to the National

180
00:06:56,831 --> 00:06:57,612
Security Guard —

181
00:06:58,302 --> 00:07:00,568
India's federal counter-terrorism force.

182
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,365
Its commandos had to fly in from Delhi.

183
00:07:05,615 --> 00:07:06,644
The city waited.

184
00:07:07,829 --> 00:07:11,319
At the Taj, NSG commandos were inside by

185
00:07:11,332 --> 00:07:13,168
around 7:20 that evening.

186
00:07:13,584 --> 00:07:14,261
Until then,

187
00:07:14,743 --> 00:07:16,215
the fighting came in bursts:

188
00:07:16,957 --> 00:07:19,548
small evacuations, sudden gunfire,

189
00:07:20,173 --> 00:07:21,801
a fire on the fourth floor.

190
00:07:22,977 --> 00:07:24,475
At the Oberoi-Trident,

191
00:07:25,034 --> 00:07:28,172
the NSG arrived around 5:30 PM.

192
00:07:28,589 --> 00:07:31,050
A naval helicopter surveyed the building

193
00:07:31,063 --> 00:07:31,870
from above.

194
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:35,113
Roughly 250 guests would be brought out of

195
00:07:35,126 --> 00:07:36,298
this hotel alone,

196
00:07:36,766 --> 00:07:38,212
in waves through the night.

197
00:07:39,356 --> 00:07:42,142
At Nariman House, there was nothing to

198
00:07:42,156 --> 00:07:43,015
negotiate.

199
00:07:43,434 --> 00:07:45,882
Two gunmen held the rabbi, his wife, and

200
00:07:45,895 --> 00:07:46,871
several others.

201
00:07:47,262 --> 00:07:49,723
The NSG would not move in until the

202
00:07:49,736 --> 00:07:50,686
following morning.

203
00:07:51,868 --> 00:07:54,173
By the end of the second day, none of the

204
00:07:54,186 --> 00:07:55,709
three sieges was over.

205
00:07:56,975 --> 00:07:59,475
Twenty-four hours, and no end in sight.

206
00:08:00,844 --> 00:08:03,631
The 28th is when two of the sieges finally

207
00:08:03,644 --> 00:08:04,047
end.

208
00:08:05,290 --> 00:08:07,504
Neither ends the way anyone hoped.

209
00:08:08,693 --> 00:08:11,545
At Nariman House, just after 7:30 in the

210
00:08:11,558 --> 00:08:12,026
morning,

211
00:08:12,599 --> 00:08:15,360
NSG commandos fast-roped from helicopters

212
00:08:15,373 --> 00:08:17,834
onto the roof, with snipers covering them

213
00:08:17,847 --> 00:08:19,110
from nearby buildings.

214
00:08:19,526 --> 00:08:20,829
It took most of the day.

215
00:08:21,271 --> 00:08:23,784
By 7:30 in the evening, they had found all

216
00:08:23,797 --> 00:08:26,428
six hostages — the rabbi and his wife

217
00:08:26,441 --> 00:08:28,941
among them — murdered by their captors.

218
00:08:29,318 --> 00:08:31,805
An hour later, both gunmen were dead,

219
00:08:32,274 --> 00:08:34,162
and the siege was declared over.

220
00:08:35,337 --> 00:08:38,306
At the Oberoi-Trident, the operation ended

221
00:08:38,319 --> 00:08:39,595
around 3:00 PM.

222
00:08:39,986 --> 00:08:41,743
Officials counted one hundred and

223
00:08:41,756 --> 00:08:44,204
forty-three hostages rescued alive.

224
00:08:44,608 --> 00:08:46,704
Twenty-four bodies were recovered.

225
00:08:47,121 --> 00:08:49,191
Both terrorists inside were killed.

226
00:08:50,352 --> 00:08:52,201
The Taj, though, was still burning.

227
00:08:52,618 --> 00:08:54,480
Six more bodies were recovered that

228
00:08:54,493 --> 00:08:55,105
afternoon,

229
00:08:55,730 --> 00:08:57,995
followed by ten grenade blasts between

230
00:08:58,008 --> 00:09:01,029
2:53 and 3:59 PM.

231
00:09:01,446 --> 00:09:02,110
By evening,

232
00:09:02,657 --> 00:09:04,935
officials believed only one gunman was

233
00:09:04,948 --> 00:09:05,925
left inside.

234
00:09:07,171 --> 00:09:07,991
They were wrong.

235
00:09:10,004 --> 00:09:12,960
In the early hours of the 29th, the Taj

236
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:13,702
shook again.

237
00:09:14,119 --> 00:09:15,343
Five explosions,

238
00:09:15,838 --> 00:09:18,585
between 3:40 and 4:10 AM.

239
00:09:19,002 --> 00:09:19,939
Ten minutes later,

240
00:09:20,577 --> 00:09:22,595
officials declared the hotel under

241
00:09:22,608 --> 00:09:23,663
government control.

242
00:09:24,938 --> 00:09:27,269
By 5:05, they had taken it back.

243
00:09:28,435 --> 00:09:29,776
At 7:30 AM,

244
00:09:30,388 --> 00:09:32,836
fire was still burning on the first floor.

245
00:09:33,253 --> 00:09:35,310
Black smoke rose from the second.

246
00:09:35,727 --> 00:09:38,240
Witnesses described a gun battle still

247
00:09:38,253 --> 00:09:38,930
under way.

248
00:09:39,347 --> 00:09:41,495
Then, sometime between 8:00  and

249
00:09:41,508 --> 00:09:42,537
8:50 AM  —

250
00:09:43,045 --> 00:09:45,531
accounts differ by minutes — commandos

251
00:09:45,545 --> 00:09:48,461
declared the hotel finally, fully secured.

252
00:09:48,852 --> 00:09:51,573
The NSG called it Operation Tornado.

253
00:09:52,751 --> 00:09:55,225
From the first shots at CST to the last

254
00:09:55,238 --> 00:09:57,842
all-clear at the Taj, the siege had run

255
00:09:57,855 --> 00:09:59,301
just under sixty hours.

256
00:10:00,678 --> 00:10:03,517
By the official count, 166 people were

257
00:10:03,530 --> 00:10:04,090
killed.

258
00:10:04,519 --> 00:10:06,551
More than a dozen of them were security

259
00:10:06,564 --> 00:10:09,506
personnel, and at least 26 were foreign

260
00:10:09,519 --> 00:10:10,235
nationals.

261
00:10:10,652 --> 00:10:12,748
More than 300 were injured.

262
00:10:13,993 --> 00:10:15,985
Nine of the ten attackers died.

263
00:10:16,402 --> 00:10:19,136
Only Ajmal Kasab survived — tried,

264
00:10:19,631 --> 00:10:22,509
convicted, and executed in 2012.

265
00:10:23,695 --> 00:10:26,768
On 12 February 2009, Pakistan's

266
00:10:26,781 --> 00:10:28,409
government admitted something for the

267
00:10:28,422 --> 00:10:29,138
first time.

268
00:10:30,397 --> 00:10:32,441
The attack had been planned and launched,

269
00:10:32,467 --> 00:10:34,798
at least in part, from Pakistani soil.

270
00:10:35,174 --> 00:10:37,804
It continued to deny any official state

271
00:10:37,817 --> 00:10:38,559
involvement.

272
00:10:38,937 --> 00:10:42,153
It denied, specifically, any role for its

273
00:10:42,166 --> 00:10:44,718
intelligence services — a role multiple

274
00:10:44,731 --> 00:10:46,671
foreign investigators have alleged.

275
00:10:47,838 --> 00:10:49,752
The city didn't wait for answers.

276
00:10:50,129 --> 00:10:52,798
CST was running again within hours.

277
00:10:53,203 --> 00:10:55,625
Leopold Café was reportedly serving

278
00:10:55,638 --> 00:10:57,434
customers within four days.

279
00:10:58,694 --> 00:11:01,116
The Taj reopened to guests within about a

280
00:11:01,129 --> 00:11:01,637
